1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,264
This program is intended for authorized F.A.L.C.O.N. personnel only.

2
00:00:08,964 --> 00:00:13,111
If you are not authorized personnel, please stop the tape now.

3
00:00:14,711 --> 00:00:15,424
Thank you.

4
00:00:19,824 --> 00:00:25,041
The Federal Agency for Liminal Classification & Occult Nature presents

5
00:00:27,041 --> 00:00:28,815
WATER SAFETY AND YOU

6
00:00:29,715 --> 00:00:33,413
A field guide to Liminal Entity L-7: The Wader.

7
00:00:36,263 --> 00:00:38,524
Section one. The poolrooms.

8
00:00:40,024 --> 00:00:43,233
Congratulations on your assignment to the poolrooms.

9
00:00:43,733 --> 00:00:49,228
The poolrooms are an endless network of tiled halls, filled with warm, still water.

10
00:00:49,928 --> 00:00:51,443
The lights are always on.

11
00:00:51,943 --> 00:00:53,698
Except where they are not.

12
00:00:56,098 --> 00:00:57,528
You are not alone here.

13
00:01:00,778 --> 00:01:03,424
Section two. Identification.

14
00:01:05,024 --> 00:01:05,969
This is The Wader.

15
00:01:06,769 --> 00:01:10,748
It stands one and a quarter times the height of an average agent.

16
00:01:11,348 --> 00:01:16,187
It has long arms. It has a very wide jaw. It has no eyes.

17
00:01:16,786 --> 00:01:18,123
It does not need them.

18
00:01:20,123 --> 00:01:25,547
The Wader lives in the water, and it cannot leave the water. Dry ground is safe.

19
00:01:27,797 --> 00:01:30,262
Section three. How it hears.

20
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:37,818
The Wader hunts by sound alone. Any disturbance of the water is heard almost instantly.

21
00:01:38,318 --> 00:01:45,542
Every sound you make in the water spreads outward as ripples. The ripples show you exactly what The Wader hears.

22
00:01:46,042 --> 00:01:47,146
Learn to read them.

23
00:01:48,846 --> 00:01:50,622
Standing still is silent.

24
00:01:51,522 --> 00:01:53,607
Wading slowly is very quiet.

25
00:01:54,507 --> 00:01:56,126
Wading quickly is loud.

26
00:01:57,026 --> 00:01:59,595
Moving your hands through the water is loud.

27
00:02:01,395 --> 00:02:04,935
Small sounds will make it come, and investigate.

28
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:09,548
Section four. Falling in.

29
00:02:10,848 --> 00:02:13,528
Most personnel losses begin with a fall.

30
00:02:15,028 --> 00:02:21,783
The higher the fall, the louder the splash. The Wader will almost always hear it, and it will charge.

31
00:02:24,483 --> 00:02:27,900
It is faster than you. Do not try to outrun it.

32
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:35,326
Do not stay where you landed. Climb out of the water. Climb all the way out.

33
00:02:35,926 --> 00:02:40,676
Holding on to a ladder, with your feet over the water, is not dry ground.

34
00:02:43,726 --> 00:02:46,712
Section five. The stillness protocol.

35
00:02:48,012 --> 00:02:51,281
If you cannot reach dry ground, stop moving.

36
00:02:51,881 --> 00:03:00,745
When you are still, The Wader only knows roughly where you are. It will search the area by feel, sweeping its long arm through the water.

37
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:09,382
Do not move your head. Do not move your body. Do not move your hands.

38
00:03:11,082 --> 00:03:16,019
Personnel who remain perfectly still survive the search seventy percent of the time.

39
00:03:17,219 --> 00:03:18,834
Remain perfectly still.

40
00:03:21,884 --> 00:03:24,501
Section six. Warning signs.

41
00:03:25,901 --> 00:03:34,718
The Wader always gives itself away. Watch the water for its wake, and for its back breaking the surface. Listen for its clicking.

42
00:03:36,218 --> 00:03:38,697
Scattered clicking means it is wandering.

43
00:03:41,697 --> 00:03:45,315
Steady clicking means it is hunting a sound. Freeze.

44
00:03:48,515 --> 00:03:51,412
Rattling clicks and teeth mean it is very close.

45
00:03:54,612 --> 00:04:00,697
If the clicking speeds up into a screech, it is charging. Get off that spot. Now.

46
00:04:06,297 --> 00:04:11,681
After you climb out, it will wait nearby. Do not go back in right away.

47
00:04:14,131 --> 00:04:16,413
Section seven. Equipment.

48
00:04:17,713 --> 00:04:23,263
Bottles may be thrown to create a distraction. The Wader will go to the nearest sound.

49
00:04:25,163 --> 00:04:29,578
In unlit sectors, use the lidar scanner. It sees through the water.

50
00:04:30,178 --> 00:04:33,861
The Wader appears in red. Your team appears in yellow.

51
00:04:34,661 --> 00:04:40,480
Each scan is a snapshot. It shows where The Wader was. Not where it is.

52
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:50,964
Generators restore power. Each generator requires two canteens of mineral water. Mineral water glows a soft teal.

53
00:04:51,664 --> 00:04:56,414
A running generator hums. The hum will cover the sound of your movements.

54
00:04:58,864 --> 00:05:01,794
Section eight. Casualty procedure.

55
00:05:03,094 --> 00:05:06,871
If a member of your team is taken, do not attempt a rescue.

56
00:05:07,771 --> 00:05:13,809
The Wader will feed for six to ten seconds. During this time, it ignores all sound.

57
00:05:14,809 --> 00:05:15,852
Use this time.

58
00:05:17,952 --> 00:05:23,124
Their equipment will float where they were taken. Recover it when it is safe.

59
00:05:24,624 --> 00:05:31,993
Taken personnel have been observed returning when the team reaches a dry room. F.A.L.C.O.N. has no explanation for this.

60
00:05:34,193 --> 00:05:36,045
Do not ask them about the water.

61
00:05:38,945 --> 00:05:39,570
Remember.

62
00:05:40,070 --> 00:05:40,983
Stay dry.

63
00:05:41,583 --> 00:05:42,552
Stay quiet.

64
00:05:43,152 --> 00:05:44,082
Stay still.

65
00:05:44,982 --> 00:05:46,217
And above all.

66
00:05:46,767 --> 00:05:47,867
Do not fall in.

67
00:05:50,567 --> 00:05:54,752
This concludes your orientation. Thank you for your service.

68
00:06:04,552 --> 00:06:08,807
It can hear yours.

